Read Light on Yoga: aims, discipline, and instruction (@yoga-light-on-yoga: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @yoga, #source @yoga-source-light) { r_2N6J2MHG3Q94HBPPNWFKQP0221
**Read:** [Iyengar’s Light on Yoga](../sources/Light-on-Yoga.lingua#r_7F91WKD2ZF75XE12E244GF0FXF), beginning with the introductory account of Yoga before proceeding through the postures, breathing instructions, and appendices. Read its explanations alongside its illustrations and numbered instructions. This short reading path uses this one book.

Identify the aims and distinctions the author makes concerning discipline, conduct, attention, body, and liberation. Keep his account of Yoga’s limbs and terminology in its own setting. Similar vocabulary in another tradition does not by itself establish the same metaphysics or goal.

For one posture description and one breathing description, distinguish the stated purpose, the sequence of instruction, the role of an illustration, and the conditions or cautions the author gives. This is a textual analysis: reading every description does not require attempting every exercise or establishing physical mastery. Do not infer a medical result from a philosophical explanation or an illustrative photograph.

**Finish:** explain the author’s account of Yoga and reconstruct how one instruction fits it, citing the relevant passages. Identify a claim needing further evidence. An optional later comparison with [the Buddhist core](../../buddhism/research/Core-completion.lingua#r_562HHAD8ARXMYG8DC0GP1QKYAT) can examine a precise similarity and difference; Buddhist study is not a prerequisite for beginning this book.

**Return:** [Yoga: an entrance through Light on Yoga](../Yoga.lingua#r_017AAD0FQ294F8HBVVTQAZ55GH).
